From 588795145de618002b66d59bf67d80d811051479 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: WGS Extract AI <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:28:23 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] Build on Linux, with call_variants on ONNX Runtime (CUDA or
 CPU) (wgsextract #719)

On Linux the port builds with GCC against shared abseil, protobuf, re2,
htslib and Boost headers from CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH (a conda environment), the
libraries ONNX Runtime links too. The Metal, Core ML and BNNS targets and the
development executables stay Apple-only; the small model's plain C++ builds
as C++.

call_variants' Linux backend (onnx_inference.{h,cc}) builds the Inception-v3
graph from the .dvw weights with ONNX Runtime's model editor API, folding each
BatchNormalization into its convolution as metal_inference.mm does, and runs
it on an NVIDIA GPU through the CUDA execution provider (FP32, TF32 off) or on
the CPU. The examples' uint8 pileups go in as they are; the graph does
upstream's (x - 128) / 128. --inference_backend is auto (CUDA when a probe run
succeeds, else the CPU), cuda (fail without it) or cpu; --cpu_threads bounds
the CPU provider, and run passes --num_shards' thread count.

Also: the executable's path from /proc/self/exe; F_NOCACHE (macOS only)
guarded; make_examples_native links the example writer it uses (GNU ld orders
static libraries); librt for shm_open; call_variants joins its writer thread
on every exit path (an error used to abort the process); --version names the
Linux build; Linux's default batch is 256 (about 3 GB of GPU memory).

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
